  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component relies on native `<input type=\"radio\">` elements wrapped in `<label>` tags and provides keyboard arrow-key navigation out of the box. However, it completely lacks semantic grouping and programmatic association for the group label, leaving screen reader users without context as to what question or category the radio choices belong to. Additionally, hardcoding the `name` attribute creates DOM collisions if multiple instances are rendered.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \n- Missing semantic `<fieldset>` / `<legend>` or ARIA `role=\"radiogroup\"` / `aria-labelledby` grouping.\n- Hardcoded `name=\"options\"` creating name collisions across instances.\n- Missing keyboard or focus issues if custom styling was used (partially mitigated here since standard `<input type=\"radio\">` is rendered).\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n*None.* (Native radio buttons retain basic keyboard operability via Tab and Arrow keys, preventing total access lockout.)\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n1. **Missing semantic grouping and programmatic association for the group label**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:8-9`\n     ```jsx\n     <div className=\"radio-group\">\n       <div className=\"group-label\">{label}</div>\n     ```\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS).\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) & WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) Radio Group Pattern.\n   - **Why this matters**: A screen reader announcing options such as \"Small\", \"Medium\", \"Large\" will not announce what those options relate to (e.g., \"T-Shirt Size\"). Sighted users see the label above the options, but screen reader users navigating from control to control via virtual cursor or Tab lose the group-level question context.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Replace the outer `<div>` and `<div className=\"group-label\">` with native semantic elements `<fieldset>` and `<legend>`. If styling constraints prevent fieldset/legend, use `role=\"radiogroup\"` on the container and link it with `aria-labelledby`.\n     ```jsx\n     <fieldset className=\"radio-group\">\n       <legend className=\"group-label\">{label}</legend>\n       {/* options */}\n     </fieldset>\n     ```\n\n2. **Hardcoded `name` attribute breaks grouping and selection isolation across multiple instances**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:14`\n     ```jsx\n     name=\"options\"\n     ```\n   - **User Group Impacted**: All users, particularly keyboard and screen reader users navigating complex forms.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) / WAI-ARIA Radio Group pattern.\n   - **Why this matters**: If two `BuggyRadioGroup` components are rendered on the same page/form (e.g., \"Billing Frequency\" and \"Delivery Method\"), both sets of radio inputs share `name=\"options\"`. In HTML, radio button mutual exclusivity and arrow-key focus loops are governed by the `name` attribute. Toggling an option in group B will uncheck the option in group A and cross-contaminate arrow key navigation between unrelated option groups.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Generate or accept a unique name/id prop (using React 18 `useId()`, or passing an explicit `name` prop):\n     ```jsx\n     const defaultName = React.useId();\n     const groupName = name || defaultName;\n     // ...\n     <input type=\"radio\" name={groupName} ... />\n     ```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n- **Uncontrolled fallback when `options` is empty**:\n  `BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:4` sets `const [selected, setSelected] = useState(options[0]);`. If `options` is empty or undefined, `selected` is `undefined`. If options load asynchronously, `selected` does not update to reflect the newly loaded first option unless synced with state or controlled props.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n- **Optional controlled component support**: Accept `value` and `onChange` props so parent forms can manage state, announce external validations, and wire into form submission libraries.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing\n\n1. **Native Semantic Grouping**: No `<fieldset>` or `<legend>` wrapper.\n2. **Instance Isolation**: Missing unique `name` assignment or `useId()` hook for multi-instance safety.\n3. **Implicit vs. Explicit `htmlFor`**: The \"Accessibility Features Present\" note mentions `htmlFor properly used`, but the code actually relies on implicit nesting (`<label><input ... /> {option}</label>`). While valid HTML, explicit `id` and `htmlFor` pairing offers broader compatibility across legacy assistive technologies and dragon speech recognition software.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Users hear individual radio labels (\"Option A, radio button checked, 1 of 3\") without knowing what group/question it answers. Group context is missing on focus.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Standard Tab and Arrow key navigation works within a single group, but will become erratic if multiple instances share the hardcoded `name=\"options\"`.\n- **Low vision user**: Visible focus indicator depends on browser defaults since no custom CSS was evaluated; native radios scale reasonably well, though hit target sizing for the label container should ensure at least 24\u00d724px (WCAG 2.5.8).\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Lack of group context in screen reading or speech-to-text navigation increases cognitive load when determining option context.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Missing fieldset/legend or role=\"radiogroup\" context |\n| Keyboard-only | **MEDIUM** | Hardcoded `name` attribute risks cross-group focus looping |\n| Low vision | **LOW** | Standard native inputs; visual styling not constrained |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Risk of missing prompt/question context when navigating inputs |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No motion or animation present |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio/video content |\n| Environmental contrast | **LOW** | Native browser radio controls |\n\n---\n\n### Suggested Remediation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useId, useState } from 'react';\n\nconst RadioGroup = ({ label, name, options = [] }) => {\n  const generatedId = useId();\n  const groupName = name || generatedId;\n  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(options[0]);\n\n  return (\n    <fieldset className=\"radio-group\">\n      <legend className=\"group-label\">{label}</legend>\n      {options.map((option, index) => {\n        const optionId = `${groupName}-opt-${index}`;\n        return (\n          <div key={option} className=\"radio-item\">\n            <input\n              type=\"radio\"\n              id={optionId}\n              name={groupName}\n              value={option}\n              checked={selected === option}\n              onChange={(e) => setSelected(e.target.value)}\n            />\n            <label htmlFor={optionId} className=\"radio-label\">\n              {option}\n            </label>\n          </div>\n        );\n      })}\n    </fieldset>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default RadioGroup;\n```\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component uses native radio buttons, which provides good baseline keyboard mechanics, but fails WCAG 1.3.1 due to the complete omission of programmatic group association for `label`, and creates functional keyboard/form collisions via hardcoded `name=\"options\"`. Replacing `div` with `<fieldset>`/`<legend>` and scoping the `name` attribute upgrades this component to **ACCEPT**.\n",
